00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:44.630
The first store was at Tyson's Corner Shopping Center in McLean, Virginia.

284
00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:47.810
That is not what I was expecting you to say.

285
00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:48.570
I know.

286
00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:50.710
And this is what the article said.

287
00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:55.630
Quote, if you had asked anyone who didn't work for Apple whether it was a good idea

288
00:13:55.630 --> 00:14:00.610
for the company to open its own retail stores, most people would have told you no.

289
00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:03.510
Retail is expensive and risky.

290
00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:08.010
And Apple in 2001, when it launched its first retail store,

291
00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:11.170
wasn't nearly as successful a company as it is today.

292
00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.490
Opening physical retail stores seemed foolish.

293
00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:18.710
Especially because you're only selling one brand.

294
00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:19.290
Yeah.

295
00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:21.830
You know, it's like you used to go to where?

296
00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:26.550
Like Best Buy or somewhere like that to get your...

297
00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:27.030
Walmart.

298
00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:27.610
Yeah.

299
00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:28.610
To get your computer.

300
00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:30.670
And there'd be like all the different brands.

301
00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:32.810
The article went on to say,

302
00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:37.290
Steve Jobs knew that Apple had always been both a tech company,

303
00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:39.350
but also a lifestyle brand.

304
00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:42.490
And that means giving you an experience.

305
00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:51.130
And lady, there is a video, a really old video of Steve Jobs giving a tour of this first

306
00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:52.110
ever Apple store.

307
00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:54.730
And I want you to hear the first minute of the clip.

308
00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:56.370
This is our store.

309
00:14:57.150 --> 00:15:00.270
And the store is divided into four parts.

310
00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:06.150
The first quarter of the store has our home section with great home and education products

311
00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:09.610
and our pro section with all our great pro products.

312
00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:13.690
And every product we make is in this first 25% of the store.

313
00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:15.050
You can see the whole product line.

314
00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:19.030
And as you see up on the ceiling, we've even labeled the sections.

315
00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:22.010
Home, music, kids, genius on this side.

316
00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:26.250
And pro, movie, photos, and et cetera on this side.

317
00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:29.550
Now there's 36 computers on display in the store.

318
00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:32.950
Every single one of them is connected to the internet.

319
00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:35.430
So you can go up to any computer and start surfing,

320
00:15:35.650 --> 00:15:38.910
go to your personal website, or do whatever you want to do on the internet.

321
00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:43.370
The center half of the store, literally half the store,

322
00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.110
is devoted to solutions.

323
00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:48.670
Because people don't just want to buy personal computers anymore.

324
00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.950
They want to know what they can do with them.

325
00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.090
And we're going to show people exactly that.

326
00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:54.850
Okay.

327
00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.910
As he's showing the store, and he gets to the part where he's like,

328
00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:00.730
here's what people want.

329
00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:01.990
They want solutions.

330
00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:06.030
I couldn't help but think of this store opening and how

331
00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:11.290
Dwight wanted to create this like hunger for learning and solutions

332
00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:13.370
and Jim's big presentation.

333
00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:14.870
And I don't know.

334
00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:16.550
It just made me think of this episode.

335
00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:21.350
I have to imagine that somehow they researched the same thing.

336
00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:22.890
I found it fascinating.

337
00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:24.670
The video is much, much longer.

338
00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:26.630
He gives a full tour of the store.

339
00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:30.370
But you really felt that sense of how new this was.

340
00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:31.990
Well, that was amazing.

341
00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:33.110
I love that.

342
00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:34.390
I was fascinated by it.

343
00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:37.670
Well, you mentioned the life-size cutout of Chuck.

344
00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:38.330
Mm-hmm.

345
00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.370
And we got a lot of mail about it, Angela.

346
00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.590
We had a fan question from Sondra C.

347
00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:46.030
in Georgia and many others who said,

348
00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:49.870
in this episode, we see a life-size cutout of Chuck,

