That Pam is aware and she's strategizing with Jim how to handle this uncomfortable

508
00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:47.930
situation.

509
00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:48.950
I agree.

510
00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:52.890
Or Jim could have just called Pam and been like, okay, it's a little awkward today, but

511
00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:54.470
you know, I'm just getting through it.

512
00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:55.070
Or something.

513
00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:55.690
Yeah, something.

514
00:23:56.250 --> 00:24:01.390
Nellie is going to go on to share that she is from a working class town named Baselton.

515
00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:02.010
Mm-hmm.

516
00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:03.390
I looked it up.

517
00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:04.010
Yep.

518
00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:06.330
It's about 26 miles from central London.

519
00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:08.630
Do you know anything about Baselton?

520
00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:09.070
I don't.

521
00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:14.610
In 1949, it was officially declared to be one of the new towns built by the government

522
00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:18.430
to rehouse Londoners whose homes were destroyed in the Blitz.

523
00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:19.810
Oh, wow.

524
00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:20.310
Yeah.

525
00:24:20.810 --> 00:24:25.530
Since then, it has developed into an iconic part of South Essex and earned itself quite

526
00:24:25.530 --> 00:24:26.230
the reputation.

527
00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:30.970
It has lots of transport connections to London, as well as a whole host of amenities.

528
00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:33.170
You know what I found very interesting?

529
00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:33.710
What?

530
00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:38.430
Claim to fame for Baselton, it is the home of the band Depeche Mode.

531
00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:39.450
Whoa.

532
00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:42.130
Yeah, they were formed in Baselton in 1980.

533
00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:43.330
I mean, come on.

534
00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:46.570
I just started listening to Depeche Mode last night after I read that.

535
00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:48.370
Do you know any Depeche Mode?

536
00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:49.610
I loved them.

537
00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:53.750
I mean, I'm sure I do, but I can't think nothing's jumping to the front of my mind.

538
00:24:53.850 --> 00:24:55.210
Let me give you just a few bars here.

539
00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:55.610
Okay.

540
00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:56.410
Just a few bars.

541
00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:59.530
When I think of Depeche Mode, I think moody.

542
00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:01.510
Am I remembering them correctly?

543
00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:03.130
Oh, no.

544
00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:04.330
That's not moody.

545
00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:05.090
That's not moody.

546
00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:06.290
That's a good time.

547
00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:11.030
Got some synthesizer?

548
00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:12.550
Oh, yeah.

549
00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:13.770
Hello, 80s.

550
00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:16.310
I mean, I've got my scrunchie.

551
00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:18.710
I mean, I've crimped my hair.

552
00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.650
Please tell me there's a saxophone break in this song.

553
00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:22.430
Oh, no, no, no.

554
00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:23.090
No saxophone.

555
00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:23.330
No.

556
00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:23.750
Darn it.

557
00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:28.700
I just can't get enough.

558
00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:30.560
I just can't get enough.

559
00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:31.100
Oh, yeah.

560
00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:31.520
Yeah.

561
00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:34.760
I mean, I love it.

562
00:25:34.760 --> 00:25:35.660
Oh, and they're cuties, too.

563
00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:36.180
I know.

564
00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:37.140
Okay.

565
00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:39.220
It took me back, guys.

566
00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:44.240
It took me back to some neon outfits, my white high tops.

567
00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:46.000
All of which are coming back.

568
00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:46.780
I know.

569
00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:53.560
Well, when Nellie is done, she's going to introduce Dwight, who's going to start handing out dossiers

570
00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:56.540
on our bloggers who are coming to the store.

571
00:25:56.540 --> 00:26:01.520
He, like you said, starts handing out different roles people are supposed to play.

572
00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:04.240
And we got a fan question from Melissa B.

573
00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:08.420
in Michigan, who said, while Dwight is giving

574
00:26:08.420 --> 00:26:12.040
instructions for the opening day, Ryan is eating.

575
00:26:12.740 --> 00:26:14.440
This has been driving me crazy.

576
00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:16.220
No one else is eating.

577
00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:22.220
And it doesn't look like prop food, like a donut or a bagel or something you might have

578
00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:24.060
on hand for the store employees.

579
00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.240
But it's also not takeout.

580
00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:27.880
What is it?

581
00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:30.560
Was this a scripted snack?

582
00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:33.420
And if so, why is he the only one eating?

583
00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:36.060
Well, Melissa, I looked.

584
00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:39.260
There is nothing in the script that says Ryan is eating.

585
00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:40.680
And I zoomed in on it.

586
00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:47.720
And it honestly looks like BJ stopped by craft services and grabbed his plate onto the set.

587
00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:49.780
It looks like crafty to me.

588
00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:50.100
Yeah.

589
00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:53.060
I mean, sometimes the fellows would do this.

590
00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:54.520
Yes, this is true.

591
00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:57.340
We also had a fan catch from Grace L.

592
00:26:57.540 --> 00:26:59.160
in British Columbia who said,

593
00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:04.620
did you notice at two minutes and 29 seconds, Aaron slaps Kathy's hand?

594
00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:06.200
No.

595
00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:07.520
Yeah.

596
00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:09.220
I did not notice that.

597
00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:10.920
I had to go back and look at it.

598
00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:13.020
There's nothing in the script about it.

599
00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:18.980
But, yeah, Aaron swats Kathy's hand during this Dwight speech at one point.

600
00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:19.880
Oh, wait.

601
00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:21.880
Oh, I did see that.

602
00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.980
And also in deleted scenes, she does it.

603
00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:29.420
I thought she was giving her props, like a high five, like lean over, like slap the hand.

604
00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:32.020
But Kathy doesn't reciprocate or notice.

605
00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:33.100
Yeah, I know.

606
00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:36.700
But I think Aaron's just excited because it's like towards the end of the speech.

607
00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:37.720
And she's like, heck yeah.

608
00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:39.000
Right.

609
00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:39.580
Yeah.

610
00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.820
It's very low into the side.

611
00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.440
Yeah, it's a little low slide.

612
00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:44.220
Yeah, girl.

613
00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:45.180
Okay.

614
00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:51.920
You know why I felt the need to act it out with my own hands for you guys who can't see me doing it.

615
00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:53.620
Well, it wasn't in the script.

616
00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:56.160
So I guess it was just a choice made on the day.

617
00:27:56.740 --> 00:28:00.140
Yeah, I think Ellie just improvised that moment because, you know, Nellie's like,

618
00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:02.580
she wants to create a sense of wonder, enthusiasm.

619
00:28:03.380 --> 00:28:04.900
She wants goose pimples.

620
00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:07.340
And now everyone let in the bloggers.

621
00:28:07.340 --> 00:28:12.540
I just realized I haven't given y'all a location breakdown for this store.

622
00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:14.440
Jenna, what's going on?

623
00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:15.580
No location breakdown?

624
00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:17.040
I mean, I have one.

625
00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:17.840
That's the thing.

626
00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:18.160
Oh.

627
00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:18.820
Of course.

628
00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:22.580
I emailed Steve Burgess and asked him, where did we shoot this store?

629
00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:26.740
I wondered if the back of the store was a set.

630
00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:29.280
Obviously, the front of the store is a real location.

631
00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:30.080
The break room.

632
00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:30.560
Mm-hmm.

633
00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:34.440
You know, because we built the hotel conference room on our stages.

634
00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:34.860
Oh, yeah.

635
00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:39.920
So I thought maybe to control light and sound, did we build the back room?

636
00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:40.920
We didn't.

637
00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:42.480
The answer is no.

638
00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:42.940
We didn't.

639
00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:43.700
We didn't.

640
00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:44.320
No.

641
00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:49.420
No, we shot the whole thing, the back room, the front room in an abandoned mini mall.

642
00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:54.660
Steve said there were only a couple of stores still open in this mall, but it was mostly empty.

643
00:28:54.660 --> 00:28:57.640
So we really had to dress more than one storefront.

644
00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:02.140
And then we used the other empty storefronts, like, to store our equipment.

645
00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:02.840
Mm-hmm.

646
00:29:02.980 --> 00:29:03.680
Stuff like that.

647
00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:06.180
So we sort of took over an old abandoned mini mall.

648
00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:06.820
Yes.

649
00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:08.340
And it was in Valencia.

650
00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:09.080
Okay.

651
00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:09.560
Mm-hmm.

652
00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:11.620
I love a location breakdown.

653
00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:13.800
I like to know all the details.

654
00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:22.540
Before we move out of this break room, we got a fan mail flurry about the most interesting detail.

655
00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:24.660
I don't know if you noticed it.

656
00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:27.240
Is it a green monkey that sits on the couch?

657
00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:27.760
Yes!

658
00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:27.840
Yes!

659
00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:28.780
The green monkey!

660
00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:29.720
What the heck?

661
00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:30.920
What the heck?

662
00:29:31.060 --> 00:29:32.940
Also, Isabel has this monkey.

663
00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:34.460
She does?

664
00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:35.060
Yes.

665
00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:37.840
I can tell you where this monkey is from.

666
00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:39.400
Thanks to Aaron M.

667
00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:40.140
from New Jersey.

668
00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:40.540
Okay.

669
00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:42.040
Aaron M.

670
00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:48.600
wrote in and said, I have been waiting for this episode to ask you because I have this exact same monkey.

671
00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:55.520
Aaron says, you know how everyone has that one special stuffed animal that they latched onto as a kid and they took everywhere?

672
00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:59.120
And then it gets kind of beat up and falling apart, but you will never get rid of it?

673
00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:00.920
Yes, I still have my bear, Henry.

674
00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:03.180
I still have my dog, Prancy.

675
00:30:06.280 --> 00:30:14.380
Well, Aaron said, mine is this monkey who I named Steve, and I got him a Pier 1 import store when I was a kid.

676
00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:16.080
Oh, my gosh, Aaron.

677
00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:17.900
It is all flooding back to me.

678
00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:22.360
There was a Pier 1 by my house when I was filming The Office.

679
00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:24.940
I got my daughter this monkey at Pier 1.

680
00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:25.960
I forgot.

681
00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:32.200
Aaron said that her parents were furniture shopping and Steve was her reward for being patient while they looked at furniture.

682
00:30:33.240 --> 00:30:40.200
Aaron slept with Steve every night, brought him on vacations, and I even used to buy baby clothes and put them on him.

683
00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:41.720
He was my best buddy.

684
00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:49.800
Aaron goes on to say, years later, as a teenager, I worked at the same Pier 1, but no more green monkey.

685
00:30:50.200 --> 00:30:51.860
They had been discontinued.

686
00:30:51.860 --> 00:30:59.220
So I have no idea how or why this green monkey ended up on this couch, but it is the Pier 1 green monkey.

687
00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:00.760
His name was Max.

688
00:31:01.060 --> 00:31:02.560
That's what Pier 1 called him.

689
00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:14.100
And it is not for sale anymore, but there are tons, like tons of them posted on places like eBay or Mercari, like used monkeys, because clearly this monkey was a big hit.

690
00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:18.000
So you can go on and get one for like $50, $35.

691
00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:19.720
It's really funny.

692
00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:23.960
There's one moment where Jim is sitting on the couch and the monkey is just sitting next to him.

693
00:31:24.020 --> 00:31:24.240
Yeah.

694
00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:27.580
Like perfectly positioned as if they're both just hanging out.

695
00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:28.520
And I'm like, what?

696
00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:30.220
What were we doing?

697
00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:31.780
What was Phil Shea up to?

698
00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:33.400
Were they just having some fun?

699
00:31:33.540 --> 00:31:35.420
I looked back at the shooting draft.

700
00:31:35.680 --> 00:31:37.620
I looked back at all of the alts.

701
00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:39.020
I couldn't find anything.

702
00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:40.160
We never used the monkey.

703
00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:40.600
Nope.

704
00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:41.980
Yeah, there it is.

705
00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:44.240
I wish I could get down to it.

706
00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:47.720
I asked Brent about it, but he didn't have anything to say about the monkey either.

707
00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:48.980
We don't know how it goes.

708
00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:49.460
We don't know.

709
00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:50.620
But we know what it is.

710
00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:55.240
Well, let's take a break, because when we get back, Andy's got a big old black eye.

711
00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:56.620
He does.

712
00:31:56.840 --> 00:31:58.220
And a story to go with it.

713
00:31:58.420 --> 00:32:11.000
We are back, and Andy has arrived to work.

714
00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:12.040
He's walking in with Pam.

715
00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:15.020
He's kind of hiding his face, trying to get into his office quickly.

716
00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:19.320
But people need his attention, so he's going to have to reveal.

717
00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:23.680
I mean, Kevin, in particular, needs everyone to know that, guess what?

718
00:32:24.420 --> 00:32:26.100
The paper boy isn't a paper boy.

719
00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:26.520
No.

720
00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:28.160
No, it was a man in a car.

721
00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:28.580
Yeah.

722
00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:29.260
Big news.

723
00:32:29.260 --> 00:32:30.140
Not a kid on a bike.

724
00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:31.220
Not a kid on a bike.

725
00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:36.620
This news is not hitting everyone the way Kevin hoped it would, because Andy turns around,

726
00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:39.520
and we see his black eye.

727
00:32:39.940 --> 00:32:41.040
It's not just his eye.

728
00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:42.140
It's like his whole cheek.

729
00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:45.780
I had to Google something when I saw it.

730
00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:49.060
Is it about getting hit in the face, and what happens to your eye, or something?

731
00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:49.780
Yeah.

732
00:32:50.020 --> 00:32:51.340
Everyone is shocked.

733
00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:53.500
And they're like, what happened?

734
00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:54.100
What happened?

735
00:32:54.780 --> 00:33:02.640
And it turns out that there was a gang who was harassing Pam in the parking lot with weapons.

736
00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:03.260
Yeah.

737
00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:06.800
And Andy stepped in to confront them, and punches were thrown.

738
00:33:07.060 --> 00:33:07.400
Yeah.

739
00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:10.160
And one landed right on Andy's face.

740
00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:10.580
Yep.

741
00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:14.760
Of course, during this whole speech, we see what really happened.

742
00:33:14.760 --> 00:33:16.840
We see some documentary footage.

743
00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:20.420
Yeah, of like a group of kids on bicycles.

744
00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:23.580
They throw a bunch of, it looks like pine cones.

745
00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:25.060
They have pine cones in their basket.

746
00:33:25.500 --> 00:33:29.200
And then Andy steps in, because they're throwing pine cones at Pam,

747
00:33:29.380 --> 00:33:31.980
and then this young girl punches Andy in the face.

748
00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:33.740
And then they all ride away on their bicycles.

749
00:33:34.040 --> 00:33:34.320
Yes.

750
00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:37.040
Does your eye get that bruised?

751
00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:40.620
Immediately after getting punched in the eye.

752
00:33:40.720 --> 00:33:45.080
I know we had to have it because we needed this visual, you know.

753
00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:46.200
I don't know.

754
00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:47.800
Look at this big bruise I have on my elbow.

755
00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:49.180
That's like five days old.

756
00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:50.020
I know.

757
00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:50.780
That's what I mean.

758
00:33:50.800 --> 00:33:52.600
I actually looked it up.

759
00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:57.580
It says it takes one to two days to get a black and blue bruise after an injury.

760
00:33:57.820 --> 00:33:58.280
Well.

761
00:33:58.280 --> 00:33:59.660
It bruises up in time.

762
00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:02.460
Well, that's some TV magic for you.

763
00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:03.140
Yeah.

764
00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:04.560
A wound check.

765
00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:05.980
I did a little wound check.

766
00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:06.780
Wound check.

767
00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:09.300
Oscar thinks they should call the police.

768
00:34:09.640 --> 00:34:10.840
Everyone's in agreement with them.

